.Dd September 16, 2026
.Dt NDI_DEV_IS_PROM_NODE 9F
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ndi_dev_is_prom_node ,
.Nm ndi_dev_is_pseudo_node ,
.Nm ndi_dev_is_persistent_node ,
.Nm ndi_dev_is_hotplug_node ,
.Nm ndi_dev_is_hidden_node ,
.Nm ndi_devi_set_hidden ,
.Nm ndi_devi_clr_hidden
.Nd inspect device-node origin and visibility attributes
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/sunndi.h
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_dev_is_prom_node
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fc
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_dev_is_pseudo_node
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fc
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_dev_is_persistent_node
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fc
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_dev_is_hotplug_node
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fc
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_dev_is_hidden_node
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fc
.Ft "void"
.Fo ndi_devi_set_hidden
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fc
.Ft "void"
.Fo ndi_devi_clr_hidden
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fc
.Sh INTERFACE LEVEL
illumos specific
.Pq Consolidation Private .
.Sh PARAMETERS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Va dip
Valid device node whose attributes are accessed.
.El
.Sh DESCRIPTION
\fBndi_dev_is_prom_node()\fP tests whether the node belongs to the PROM class.
\fBndi_dev_is_pseudo_node()\fP tests the software-created class.
All non-PROM nodes are pseudo nodes, including persistent nodes created by
hardware enumeration; pseudo does not mean nonpersistent.
.Pp
\fBndi_dev_is_persistent_node()\fP tests the persistence attribute,
\fBndi_dev_is_hotplug_node()\fP tests the hotplug-origin attribute, and
\fBndi_dev_is_hidden_node()\fP tests visibility.
The allocation node identifier determines the initial attributes, as described
in \fBndi_devi_alloc(9F)\fP.
.Pp
\fBndi_devi_set_hidden()\fP and \fBndi_devi_clr_hidden()\fP set and clear the hidden
attribute used by device lookup and snapshots.
They do not acquire locks, change attachment state, invalidate caches, or
generate events.
Callers must serialize attribute changes using the device-node locking
protocol and keep the node alive throughout access.
For physical removal and reinsertion notification, use
\fBndi_devi_device_remove()\fP and \fBndi_devi_device_insert()\fP.
.Sh CONTEXT
These functions may be called from user, kernel, or interrupt context
provided the caller supplies the necessary lifetime and synchronization
protection.
They do not allocate memory or acquire locks.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The query functions return nonzero when the tested attribute is
present, and zero otherwise.
The set and clear functions have no return value.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ndi_devi_alloc 9F ,
.Xr ndi_devi_device_remove 9F ,
.Xr sunndi 9F
